  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1113 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nOn Thursday and Friday, a weak upper-level shortwave trough will \nmove into south Texas then to the gulf coast Friday night. Winds at \nthe surface will generally be from the southeast which should allow \nwarm conditions will continue across the region. High temperatures \nshould reach the mid to upper 80s each afternoon while some \nlocations in the Big Country could reach the low 90s. A few \nafternoon showers could occur over the Edwards Plateau late Thursday \nnight into Friday morning.  However, these will be fairly spotty so \nPoPs were kept under 20 percent during this time.\n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, global models are in better agreement \nof Tropical Depression Nine strengthening and moving northward out \nof the Bay of Campeche and into the Mississippi Delta. Although this \nsystem is forecast to become a hurricane by early Friday morning, it \nshould have little to no impact on the weather for west central \nTexas this weekend. Rather, the aforementioned upper-level trough\nlooks to be quickly absorbed by this system as it makes landfall \non Saturday morning. In its wake, a broad ridge should build in \nacross east Texas and the western gulf for the rest of the \nweekend. Meanwhile, a larger scale trough is expected to dig \nsouthward along the west coast by Sunday night and move gradually \neast through Tuesday. This should allow southwesterly flow aloft \nto build in across far west Texas and the Panhandle. West-central \nTexas will be in between these two primary upper-level features \nfor the first part of next week. As a result, above-normal \ntemperatures should continue with highs in the upper 80s to around\n90. However, there is a low (<20%) chance for rain for the early \nto middle part of next week, depending on how this next system \nevolves.",
